  "html": "<p>This open-access dataset (Version 2.0) examines how young minds\nconstruct meaning through visual design and spontaneous communication.\nUtilizing a creative \"Firefly\" protocol, the study captures responses\nfrom 89 students (ages 3–15) within the Blue Blocks ecosystem.\nParticipants were tasked with assigning functional colors to three\nimagined biological light signals—<em>Glow</em>, <em>Hello</em>, and\n<em>Danger</em>—before selecting an animal interlocutor and composing a\ndirect, unedited message to them.</p>\n<p>The resulting archive provides crucial foundational data for research\ninto child development, color cognition, visual semiotics, and\nplay-based learning. Preliminary patterns within the data reveal\ndistinct cognitive associations, notably a strong, uniform tendency to\nassign warm colors (specifically red) to the \"Danger\" signal, alongside\nhigh levels of narrative engagement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dataset-architecture-1\"><strong>Dataset\nArchitecture</strong></h2>\n<p>Engineered for immediate quantitative and qualitative analysis, the\nV2.0 workbook synthesizes raw child responses with a comprehensive suite\nof derived variables. It includes a full data dictionary and seven live,\nformula-driven analysis sheets (covering descriptive statistics,\ncross-tabulations, and thematic analyses) that update dynamically as\ndata is queried.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Verbatim Records:</strong> The unedited capture of\nchildren’s original free-text color choices, spontaneous animal\nselections (including phonetic/unresolved spellings), and exact message\nphrasing.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Derived Analytical Variables:</strong> Researcher-coded\ncategorizations including normalized primary colors, multi-color flags,\nthermal/warmth classifications for each signal, banded age groups, and\ncategorized message themes.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"methodological-ethical-framework\"><strong>Methodological &amp;\nEthical Framework</strong></h2>\n<p>The dataset utilizes a rule-based coding procedure for initial animal\nand message categorizations. To support rigorous secondary publication,\nit is structured to easily integrate a second independent coder for\nestablishing inter-rater reliability (e.g., Cohen’s Kappa).</p>\n<p>Adhering to strict privacy protocols, all direct identifiers have\nbeen stripped. The architecture relies on unique, non-identifying Row\nIDs to manage data integrity, including customized tracking for students\nwho completed the activity multiple times or required temporary source\ncodes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"access-application-1\"><strong>Access &amp;\nApplication</strong></h2>\n<p>Published as an analysis-ready archival record, this dataset is\ndesigned for researchers investigating how young learners map aesthetic\nchoices onto communicative functions and conceptualize empathy across\nspecies.</p>\n<p><strong>Access the Data:</strong> Open Dataset (Version 2.0)- <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22124612\"><u>https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22124612</u></a></p>",
